Hamnix Micro feather-nrf52840 size patch to user/progs/exportfs.ad.
Applied by scripts/mkroot.sh in build/feather-nrf52840/root only; the
committed file is unchanged.

Reason: the nRF52840 has 256 KiB of RAM for everything: kernel data, kernel
stacks and heap, the files in / and every running program's stack and data
(boards/feather_nrf52840/link.ld; programs' code stays in flash).

    request and reply buffers   4120 bytes each -> 1048 bytes each
    a directory's listing       2048 bytes -> 1024 bytes
exportfs then needs about 6 KiB of data instead of 13 KiB.  A client is
told the smaller message size when it connects; a directory with more than
1024 bytes of names is served cut short.

Only constants, array lengths, and messages that quote them change.
Generated by editing a copy and
    diff -u --label a/user/progs/exportfs.ad --label b/user/progs/exportfs.ad user/progs/exportfs.ad <copy>

--- a/user/progs/exportfs.ad
+++ b/user/progs/exportfs.ad
@@ -39,18 +39,18 @@
 extern def sys_socketpair(domain: int32, type: int32, protocol: int32, sv: Ptr[int32]) -> int32
 extern def sys_srv_post(name: Ptr[uint8], srvfd: int32) -> int32
 
-MSIZE: uint32 = 4120                # one message: 4096 bytes of data + the largest header
+MSIZE: uint32 = 1048                # one message: 4096 bytes of data + the largest header
 DEV_MSIZE: uint32 = 280             # ... over a device: a whole request must fit a serial
                                     # driver's receive buffer before the sender is held off
                                     # (the console's is 1024 bytes, throttled at 512)
 IOHDR: uint32 = 24
 NFID: uint32 = 16
 PATHMAX: uint32 = 128
-DLIST: uint32 = 2048                # a directory's names, as the kernel lists them
+DLIST: uint32 = 1024                # a directory's names, as the kernel lists them
 
-rx: Array[4120, uint8]
-tx: Array[4120, uint8]
-dlist: Array[2048, uint8]
+rx: Array[1048, uint8]
+tx: Array[1048, uint8]
+dlist: Array[1024, uint8]
 sbuf: Array[256, uint8]
 tmp: Array[160, uint8]
 root: Array[128, uint8]
@@ -66,9 +66,9 @@
 
 rfd: int32 = 0
 wfd: int32 = 1
-msize: uint32 = 4120
+msize: uint32 = 1048
 served: uint64 = 0
-max_msize: uint32 = 4120
+max_msize: uint32 = 1048
 wire: int32 = -1                    # /dev/consctl, when the console is the transport
 
 
